WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 237: Switzerland: Armed Neutrality, the Red Cross of Henri Dunant, and the Alpine Redoubt

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.400
[Rebecca] Today, we conclude our grand epic journey through the great nations of Europe with one of the most remarkable paradoxes of modern civilization.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:26.480
[Rebecca] How a tiny, multilingual nation surrounded by warring European empires, preserved uninterrupted peace, founded global humanitarian law, and forged an impregnable fortress of freedom.

4
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:31.040
[Rebecca] The story of Swiss armed neutrality.

5
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:41.760
[George] For two centuries following the legend of William Tell, the Swiss were celebrated as the fiercest, most terrifying shock infantry on European battlefields.

6
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:52.880
[George] Armed with 18-foot pikes and heavy halberds, Swiss mercenary squares moved like human steamrollers, crushing French, Austrian, and Burgundian armies across Italy.

7
00:00:52.880 --> 00:01:02.960
[George] But on September 13, 1515, on the muddy plains of Marignano outside Milan, the Swiss myth of invincibility met a catastrophic end.

8
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:18.320
[George] In a ferocious, two-day clash against the French army of King Francis I, who deployed modern field artillery and heavy cavalry in synchronized barrages, the Swiss Pike squares were decimated by over 10,000 casualties in the Battle of the Giants.

9
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.720
[George] The slaughter of Marignano delivered a profound spiritual and strategic awakening to the Swiss cantons.

10
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.800
[George] They recognized that fighting in foreign dynastic wars would tear their multilingual confederation apart.

11
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:44.560
[George] The Swiss withdrew from expansionist European power politics, concluding an everlasting peace with France in 1516 and adopting the policy of armed neutrality that would guide their national destiny for five centuries.

12
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:56.560
[George] The Swiss halberd was a six-foot wooden polearm combining an axe blade, a heavy spear point, and an iron beak hook designed to unhorse heavily armored knights and cleave through steel helmets.

13
00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:10.280
[George] The Swiss mercenary companies, known as Risley Ufer, served foreign monarchs for centuries under strict capitulation treaties, earning a legendary reputation for unflinching courage and unwavering loyalty to their employers.

14
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:25.800
[George] For three hundred years, Swiss neutrality was a de facto national survival strategy, allowing the Confederation to remain an island of peace during the devastating religious wars of the Reformation and the Thirty Years' War, while German lands burned to ashes.

15
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:39.720
[George] But in the aftermath of the Napoleonic Wars, which had briefly seen French armies occupy Switzerland, European powers recognized that a neutral, sovereign state in the Alpine heart of Europe was essential to continental stability.

16
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:59.560
[George] On November 20, 1815, at the Congress of Vienna, the great powers, Britain, Austria, Prussia, Russia and France, signed a historic treaty formally declaring that the perpetual neutrality of Switzerland and the inviolability of its territory was in the true political interest of the whole of Europe.

17
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.760
[George] Swiss neutrality was not pacifism, but an armed, vigilant neutrality.

18
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:18.760
[George] The Swiss people made a solemn constitutional commitment that their neutrality would be defended by a formidable citizen army, guaranteeing that no foreign power could ever march through the strategic Alpine mountain passes to attack its neighbors.

19
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:30.920
[George] The Congress of Vienna recognized that without a neutral, fortified Switzerland in the center of Europe, the Alpine Mountain Passes would become permanent battlegrounds for competing Austrian and French ambitions.

20
00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:44.840
[George] The perpetual neutrality guaranteed at Vienna in 1815 provided Switzerland with the international stability necessary to build a prosperous industrial economy based on precision watchmaking, pharmaceuticals, and banking.

21
00:03:44.800 --> 00:04:00.320
[George] On June 24, 1859, a wealthy young Genevan businessman named Henry Dunant arrived in the small town of Salferino in northern Italy, seeking an audience with French Emperor Napoleon III to discuss business concessions in Algeria.

22
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:17.360
[George] Instead of imperial diplomacy, Dunant stumbled into the gruesome aftermath of the Battle of Solferino, a ferocious nine-hour clash between the Allied French Sardinian army and the Austrian Empire that left 40,000 soldiers dead, dying, or mangled upon the battlefield.

23
00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.320
[George] Medical corps were practically non existent.

24
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:31.520
[George] Thousands of wounded soldiers lay under the scorching summer sun for days without water, food or medical attention, suffering amputations without anesthesia and dying of gangrene.

25
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.200
[George] Shattered by the horror.

26
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:50.480
[George] Dunant abandoned his business mission, recruited local Italian women and peasants, and organized relief in the church of Castiglioni del Stivier, washing wounds, offering water, and caring for French, Italian, and Austrian wounded alike under the universal compassionate motto.

27
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.360
[George] Tuti fratelli, we are all brothers.

28
00:04:53.360 --> 00:05:04.240
[George] Dunant spent days in the sweltering heat of the Castiglioni church, bathing the gangrenous wounds of young soldiers who wept and cried out for their mothers in French, German, and Italian.

29
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:17.280
[George] In Castiglioni, Dunant organized teams of local women who provided clean water, soup, and bandages to wounded soldiers lying on the stone floors, treating friends and foes with equal human dignity.

30
00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.920
[George] Returning to Geneva, Dunant was haunted by the memories of Solferino.

31
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:32.440
[George] In 1862, he published a passionate, groundbreaking book entitled A Memory of Solferino, distributing it to monarchs, generals and statesmen across Europe.

32
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.320
[George] Dunant asked two revolutionary questions.

33
00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:51.080
[George] Could nations form voluntary, neutral relief societies in peacetime to care for the wounded in time of war, and could European powers formulate an international, inviolable agreement granting neutral legal protection to military medical personnel on the battlefield.

34
00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:54.280
[George] Dunant's appeals struck a deep moral cord.

35
00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:12.520
[George] In 1863, Dunant and four prominent Genevan citizens, including Gustave Moynier and General Guillaume Henry Dufer, founded the International Committee of the Red Cross, adopting the iconic emblem of a Red Cross on a white background, the exact inverse of the Swiss national flag.

36
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:27.960
[George] In August 1864, sixteen nations gathered in Geneva to sign the first Geneva Convention, creating the foundational cornerstone of modern international humanitarian law and turning neutral Switzerland into the moral conscience of humanity.

37
00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:40.200
[George] The Red Cross emblem was chosen specifically to pay homage to Switzerland, reversing the colors of the Swiss federal banner to create a universal, recognizable symbol of humanitarian sanctuary.

38
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:55.480
[George] The International Committee of the Red Cross was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize three times in 1917, 1944, and 1963, honoring its relentless humanitarian service across the darkest conflicts of modern history.

39
00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:00.920
[George] The greatest existential test of Swiss independence occurred during the Second World War.

40
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:10.280
[George] By the summer of 1940, following the rapid fall of France, Switzerland found itself completely surrounded by Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy.

41
00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.920
[George] Adolf Hitler despised Switzerland.

42
00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:29.440
[George] Privately deriding it as a pimple on the face of Europe, whose multi-ethnic democracy was an insult to the Nazi Reich, developing secret invasion plans codenamed Operation Tannenbaum that allocated 21 German and Italian divisions to dismember the Swiss Confederation.

43
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:38.720
[George] Confronted with the greatest military colossus in history, the Swiss Federal Council and Commander-in-Chief General Henry Gisson refused to surrender.

44
00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:50.240
[George] Every Swiss man aged 18 to 60 was mobilized into the militia army, keeping their assault rifles, ammunition, and uniforms in their homes, ready to assemble within hours.

45
00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.240
[George] General Gisson issued a secret, unalterable directive.

46
00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:02.800
[George] If radio broadcasts ever claimed that Switzerland had surrendered, citizens were to treat it as hostile propaganda and fight on to the death.

47
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:15.680
[George] Every Swiss household received an official civil defense handbook detailing food stockpilling, gas mask drills, and marksmanship practice, turning the entire population into a permanent citizen army.

48
00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:27.600
[George] Every Swiss male citizen completes compulsory military recruit school and annual refresher courses, maintaining his service rifle and combat gear at home in readiness for national mobilization.

49
00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:42.800
[George] Recognizing that the Swiss Citizen Army could never defeat the mechanized panzer divisions and Luftwaffe of Nazi Germany on the flat, exposed Swiss plateau between Zurich and Bern, General Gisson conceived the masterstroke of Swiss defense strategy.

50
00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.080
[George] The Schwyzer Reduit, the national redoubt.

51
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:57.760
[George] On July 25, 1940, Gisson assembled all senior army commanders at the historic Rootley Meadow, standing on the very ground where the Confederation was founded in 1291.

52
00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.720
[George] Gisson announced an audacious, uncompromising strategy.

53
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:14.280
[George] If the Wehrmacht invaded, the Swiss army would execute a fighting retreat, abandoning the industrial cities of the lowlands and withdraw into the soaring granite fortresses of the high Alps around the Gotthard, St.

54
00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.840
[George] Morris, and Sargon's massifs.

55
00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:31.240
[George] For years, Swiss engineers had hollowed out entire mountain peaks, carving underground barracks, hospitals, ammunition depots, aircraft hangars, and heavy artillery batteries hidden behind false chalet walls and granite rockfaces.

56
00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:43.880
[George] Furthermore, every major bridge, railway tunnel and mountain pass, including the vital Gotthard and Simplon railway tunnels connecting Germany and Italy, was pre-wired with thousands of tons of dynamite.

57
00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.600
[George] Gisson made it crystal clear to Berlin.

58
00:09:46.600 --> 00:10:03.320
[George] The moment a German soldier stepped across the border, the transit corridors between Germany and Italy would be permanently blown to dust, and the German army would face a bloody, grinding guerrilla war in the freezing alpine snows that would cost hundreds of thousands of German lives.

59
00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:05.000
[George] The deterrence worked.

60
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:09.400
[George] Operation Tannenbaum was repeatedly postponed and never launched.

61
00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:22.120
[George] The fortress installations inside the Gotthard Mountain were engineered to be completely self-sustaining for six months, with subterranean water reservoirs, bakeries, diesel generators, and surgical suites.

62
00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:34.280
[George] The national redoubt's subterranean fortifications included artillery batteries concealed inside fake chalets whose roofs could slide open in seconds to fire heavy howitzers across alpine passes.

63
00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] In the decades following World War II, Switzerland's commitment to armed neutrality evolved into a vital instrument of international diplomacy and humanitarian service.

64
00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:59.680
[George] Geneva became the European headquarters of the United Nations, the World Health Organization, and the High Commissioner for Human Rights, operating as a trusted, neutral broker with no colonial baggage and no imperial ambitions.

65
00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:14.080
[George] Switzerland performed good offices in dozens of international conflicts, representing American diplomatic interests in Iran, mediating Cold War summits between Reagan and Gorbachev, and hosting peace negotiations across the globe.

66
00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:28.480
[George] Today, Switzerland stands as a peaceful, prosperous federation of four distinct languages and 26 sovereign cantons, proving that cultural diversity, direct democracy, and armed vigilance can coexist in harmony.

67
00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:42.560
[George] As we conclude our epic European journey, Switzerland stands as an enduring beacon of freedom, neutrality, and humanitarian law, a glorious alpine testament to the triumph of human courage, community and peace.

68
00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:54.880
[George] Today, Swiss neutrality is enshrined in international law, enabling the nation to serve as a neutral diplomatic protector and global humanitarian crossroads for peaceful international dialogue.

69
00:11:54.880 --> 00:12:08.480
[George] Switzerland's policy of armed neutrality proves that true peace is not the absence of strength, but the resolute, principled determination to defend freedom, human dignity, and democratic self governance.

70
00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:16.560
[Rebecca] With episode 237, we bring to a magnificent close our 26 episode chronicle of the major nations of Europe.

71
00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:30.000
[Rebecca] From the misty monoliths of Stonehenge and the battlefields of Joan of Arc, to the Golden Halls of Versailles, the Russian Empire of Peter and Catherine, the Renaissance splendors of Italy, and the Alpine Fortresses of Switzerland.

72
00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:34.120
[Rebecca] Thank you for exploring the deep history of our civilization.

73
00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:39.400
[Rebecca] Next week, Most Fascinating Stories embarks upon an extraordinary brand new series.

74
00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:50.600
[Rebecca] The Top 50 Famous Non-President Americans, beginning with Episode 238, Benjamin Franklin, The Lightning Rod, The Printing Press, and The American Spirit.

75
00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:55.160
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to Most Fascinating Stories.

